# Code Quality

## TypeScript

- `strict` on. No `any` unless unavoidable at a boundary; prefer `unknown` + narrow.
- Prefer named types in `types/` over repeated inline object types.
- Async errors: throw `AppError` in services; controllers use `catchAsync`.

## Controllers

```ts
// ✅ thin
export const signIn = catchAsync(async (req, res) => {
  const result = await authService.signIn(req.body);
  res.cookie('accessToken', result.token, cookieOptions).json(result.response);
});

// ❌ fat — DB/hash/upload inside controller
```

## Services

- Export functions or a small module object; keep pure of Express.
- Validate invariants (membership, ownership) in services.
- Persist only through `repositories/` — never import `models/` from services.
- Return data/DTOs; let controllers map HTTP status.

## Robustness

- Never empty `catch {}`. Log with `logger`, then throw or rethrow.
- Validate input with Zod at the route boundary.
- No dual responses; one `res` path per handler.
- Auth checks before mutations.

## Scalability habits (cheap now)

- Presence/realtime helpers isolated (`services/presence.ts`) so Redis can replace later.
- No new paid infra without docs/`TECH.md` approval.
- Keep request handlers fast; heavy media work stays isolated for future workers.

## Dependencies

- Latest majors already chosen (Express 5, Mongoose 9, etc.). Don’t reintroduce `dotenv`.
- Load env via Node `--env-file` in npm scripts.
